# modules/iac/helm.rules - Helm chart checks via the pattern-rule engine
# (docs/DESIGN.md §13 step 4's container-rules half, this ticket).
#
# Path note: same frozen path table as terraform.rules (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md
# §9's `modules/iac/*.rules` row) - flat, directly under modules/iac/, never
# nested under a `rules/` subdirectory and never named `checks.rules` (that
# basename is reserved repository-wide for the §9.5 script-check schema and
# is matched first in records_schema_for_path, ahead of this row).
#
# Scope discipline, per this ticket's own text: Helm chart sources only -
# `values.yaml` and `templates/*.yaml` - never docker-compose, and never a
# bare (non-Helm) Kubernetes manifest.  docs/DESIGN.md §7/§8.2 group
# Kubernetes-manifest checks and docker-compose/Helm-values checks together
# under one future `containers.rules` concept; this pack deliberately narrows
# to the Helm half only, the same "split the monolith into single-topic
# packs, land what's ready" precedent terraform.rules already set for
# cloud.rules (§8.2's "cloud.rules terraform.rules cloudformation.rules"
# became `terraform.rules` alone, CloudFormation deferred).  A bare
# Kubernetes manifest (a raw `kind: Pod`/`kind: Deployment` file that is not
# part of a Helm chart's `templates/` directory, and is not `values.yaml`)
# and a docker-compose file are both out of scope here and MUST NOT be
# matched by any `files:` glob below - tests/suites/iac.sh's Helm section
# cross-checks both boundaries with dedicated fixtures.
#
# KNOWN GAP, stated rather than hidden: no `modules/iac/kubernetes.rules` (or
# any other Kubernetes-manifest pattern pack) exists anywhere in this
# codebase as of this ticket - confirmed against `dev`'s tip, which carries
# only `terraform.rules` under modules/iac/ before this change.  A literal
# cross-check that "the existing Kubernetes rule pack's checks do not also
# fire on a Helm template" therefore has nothing to run against yet.
# tests/suites/iac.sh's cross-check section instead proves the two boundaries
# that ARE testable today: (a) `terraform.rules` - the only other IaC pack
# that actually exists on disk - does not fire on any Helm fixture and this
# pack does not fire on any Terraform fixture, both via the `files:` glob
# alone; and (b) this pack stays silent on a docker-compose fixture despite
# docker-compose using the same "host-published port / host bind mount /
# plaintext secret" vocabulary.  Once a real Kubernetes-manifest pack ships,
# extend that same cross-check section with it directly.
#
# Engine note: `values.yaml` is genuinely YAML and `templates/*.yaml` is
# YAML-shaped text carrying embedded Go-template directives (`{{ ... }}`).
# Neither this engine nor any other module parses YAML - `docs/FOUNDATION.md`
# tension 25 and this file's own SCA sibling both note the same thing for
# their formats - so both file kinds are scanned exactly like SAST source:
# line-based pattern matching via `scan_match`/`scan_match_offsets`
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §10.3), never a real parse tree.  Every pattern below
# is written to match a single YAML `key: value` line and to tolerate a
# Go-template expression appearing elsewhere on the same or a nearby line
# without either false-negating (missing the real key) or false-positiving
# on the template expression itself (see IAC-HELM-HARDCODED_SECRET-01's
# `{`-exclusion below).
#
# Seed rules, each straight out of docs/DESIGN.md §8.2/§7's Helm-values list
# ("exposed ports, host bind mounts of sensitive paths, plaintext secrets"):
#   hostPort published on the container spec, a hostPath volume targeting a
#   sensitive host directory, and a hardcoded credential literal.

id: IAC-HELM-HOST_PORT-01
title: Container port published directly on the host network via hostPort
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-668
owasp: A02:2025
pattern: hostPort\s*:\s*[0-9]+
dialect: ere
files: values.yaml
files: templates/*.yaml
context-deny: ^\s*#\s*scoursh-allow: host-port
context-window: 1
tags: static
tags: compliance
remediation: Remove hostPort (or the NodePort service type it typically pairs with) and reach
  the workload through a ClusterIP Service plus an Ingress/LoadBalancer instead. hostPort binds
  the container port directly onto every node's host network interface, bypassing the cluster's
  own network policy and exposing the port to anything that can reach the node.
references: CWE-668

id: IAC-HELM-HOST_MOUNT-01
title: hostPath volume mounts a sensitive host directory into the container
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-668
owasp: A02:2025
pattern: path\s*:\s*["']?(/etc|/var/run/docker\.sock|/root|/proc|/sys|/home)\b
dialect: ere
files: values.yaml
files: templates/*.yaml
context-require: hostPath
context-window: 3
tags: static
tags: compliance
remediation: Remove the hostPath volume and use a PersistentVolumeClaim, ConfigMap, or Secret
  instead. Mounting /var/run/docker.sock hands the container the equivalent of root on the host
  (full control of the container runtime); mounting /etc, /root, /proc, /sys, or /home exposes
  host secrets, credentials, or kernel/process state to anything running in the pod.
references: CWE-668

id: IAC-HELM-HARDCODED_SECRET-01
title: Plaintext credential literal in a Helm chart
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-798
owasp: A07:2025
pattern: \b(password|secret|secretKey|secret_key|apiKey|api_key|access_key|token|clientSecret)\b\s*:\s*["']?[^"'\s{][^"'\n]{7,}
dialect: ere
files: values.yaml
files: templates/*.yaml
context-deny: (CHANGEME|changeme|EXAMPLE|example|XXXXXXXX|xxxxxxxx|placeholder|dummy|TODO)
context-window: 0
tags: static
remediation: Remove the literal credential from values.yaml/templates and rotate it immediately;
  a committed secret must be treated as compromised even if the commit is later reverted, because
  it remains in git history. Source the value from an existing Kubernetes Secret via
  valueFrom.secretKeyRef (or an external-secrets operator backed by Vault/AWS Secrets Manager/SSM
  SecureString) instead of a literal in values.yaml or a template.
references: CWE-798
